<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5520"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Whoisbtk<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Whoisbtk<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, January 31, 2005 - 2:06 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Kechi  <BR>“Kechi” is a derivative of “Kichai”, the name of a local Indian tribe that used the area for camping in the 1800’s.  “Kichai” translates as “Water Turtle”.  Kechi was a stopover on the Chisholm Trail, and today local businesses use this in their names: Chisholm Creek Kennels, Chisholm Trail Animal Hospital, etc. <BR> <BR>Founded in 1888, Kechi was incorporated in 1957as a third class city &#40;a legal tag defining what powers the city may exercise.&#41;  It remains a city today.  It is not a suburb of Wichita; it has it’s own mayor, Ed Parker, and it’s own police force &#40;2 cops.&#41;  The Police Chief, Mike Morland, is very proud of his Corvette patrol car.  Here’s a link to a picture of the car and the Chief:  <a href="http://skyways.lib.ks.us/towns/Kechi/" target=_top>http://skyways.lib.ks.us/towns/Kechi/</a>. The principal municipal revenue is speeding tickets, so be careful if you visit.   <BR> <BR>Other than tickets there are some unique businesses.  There is a large collection of antique shops, a surprisingly good live theater “Kechi Playhouse”, and Karg Art Glass &#40;beautiful hand blown glass&#41; <BR> <BR>There is no Quick Trip in Kechi.  There’s one in three miles to the west in Park City. <BR> <BR>Location from Wichita &#40;this gets funky&#41; <BR> <BR>Wichita is laid out on a NSEW grid.  Main Street is the NS street, and Douglas is the EW axis; the zero/zero intersection is Main and Douglas.  Within the city EW streets crossing Main are likely to be Numbers and NS streets crossing Douglas are usually names.  Once outside the Wichita city limits, all streets are numbered based on the number of blocks from Main &#40;if they are EW&#41; or Douglas &#40;if they are NS&#41;.  <BR> <BR>This leads to some confusion.  In theory there are four “147th Street.  For example, if you drive 147 blocks south on Main from Douglas you are at 147th Street South.  Turn left and in two blocks you come to 201 east 147th Street South. <BR> <BR>Now, retrace your route to Main and Douglas; then drive east on Douglas 147 blocks and you are at 147th Street East.  Turn right onto 147th Street East from Douglas, and in two blocks you come to “201 south 147th Street East” <BR> <BR>Once you get outside Wichita city limits, Sedgwick County carries on this same street numbering convention.  County roads are laid at one-mile intervals, as one of the basic measures of land is a “Section” which is one mile square.  Each mile stretch equals 8 blocks.  So as you go north on Hillside from WSU you pass 21st, 29th, 37th, 45th, 53d, and 61st.  You are now at the center of Kechi.  Kechi is separated from Wichita by about one and a half miles of farmland.  If you look at a map, Hwy 245 is really where Kechi starts, and it ends around 69th Street North.  It’s an irregular oval 1 ½ to 2 miles in diameter <BR> <BR>When you get in Butler County, the same mile grid continues, but the streets are renumbered based on whatever Butler County has as the zero/zero point.  <BR> <BR>Geographic Significance of Kechi <BR> <BR>What does this have to do with Kechi?   61st North is the main east/west street in Kechi; Oliver is the main north/south street.  Within it’s city limits, a 3rd class city may rename county roads, and so for two miles, 61st Street North becomes Kechi Road. <BR> <BR>Start at the Oliver/Kechi Road intersection.  Drive one mile east on Kechi Road and you intersect Woodlawn where it crosses over Hwy 254.  Hwy 254 is four lanes divided, with a 70mph speed limit.  You are about ten minutes drive from &#42;Benton&#42; to the east.  If you turn left &#40;north&#41; onto Woodlawn from Kechi Road, in about one mileyou come to &#42;B&amp;K Storage&#42;, two miles from the center of Kechi.   <BR> <BR>Now go back to the Oliver/Kechi Road intersection.  Go west on Kechi Road one mile and you are at the &#42;Davis residence&#42;, one mile from the center of Kechi.  Continue west another mile and a third and you are at the &#42;Hedge residence&#42;, 2 1/3 miles from the center of Kechi.  Go another 2/3 of a mile to Broadway, turn north one mile to 69th, then west one mile to Seneca and you are at the &#42;cereal box&#42;; five miles from the center of Kechi.   <BR> <BR>Back to Oliver/Kechi Road.  Go south on Oliver one mile to 53d Street North. Turn left &#40;east&#41;, go three miles and stop.  &#42;Hedge’s body&#42; was found here: on 53d Street North just east of Webb Rd, four miles from the center of Kechi.  Continue three miles east then two miles south and stop.  This is where &#42;Hedge’s purse&#42; was found, nine miles from the center of Kechi. <BR> <BR>Back to Oliver/Kechi Road.  Go south on Oliver five miles to 21st.  Turn left &#40;east&#41; one mile.  You are at Brittany Center parking lot where the &#42;Davis car&#42; was found, six miles from the center of Kechi. <BR> <BR>Back to Oliver/Kechi Road.  Go south on Oliver six miles to 13th.  Go left &#40;east&#41; ½ mile to Edgemoor, turn right &#40;south&#41; about ½ mile and you are at the &#42;Otero home&#42;.  Seven miles from the center of Kechi.  If you don’t turn on 13th in one mile you are at the &#42;Dillons parking lot&#42; where the Otero car was found.  Seven miles. <BR> <BR> <BR> <BR>Anyone up for some antique shopping in Kechi? <BR> <BR> <BR><a href="mailto:whoisbtk@yahoo.com">whoisbtk@yahoo.com</a> <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 5520-->
