<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5799"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:Iwillc@tchu.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Catcher<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Catcher<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, February 02, 2005 - 2:47 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Wade, if you have read the the entire post, as well as some of the other areas concerning theories, you can relate to the idea that Whoisbtk is trying to infer.  It is an ongoing &#34;brainstorm&#34; of information, including names/places/roads and other erroneous data that assist in geographic profiling attempts.  Sometimes, it&#39;s just plain wierd when analyzed, so we share our findings here.  Hope this helps!<!--/Text--></td></tr>
